WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:17.280
ORACLE VS GOOGLE:
When a company is paying you tens of millions per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.880
year if not hundreds of millions, it’s probably&nbsp;
a good idea to be on their good side. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.640
it looks like Oracle didn’t have this common&nbsp;
sense as they would go ahead and sue Google in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.940
2010. If this was for some sort of serious issue&nbsp;
like a breach of contract or unethical practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:42.960
this would’ve been completely understandable. But,&nbsp;
this related to a petty copyright issue regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.260
an open source programming language called Java.&nbsp;
Essentially, Oracle was claiming that while Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:54.480
itself was free and opensource, Google’s use&nbsp;
of Java was violating Oracle’s intellectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.420
property rights. And the amount that Oracle&nbsp;
was trying to claim? A disgusting $9 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:07.500
At first, you may be swayed to give Oracle a&nbsp;
chance because hey, it’s not like Google is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.500
saint themselves. But, here’s the thing, this&nbsp;
is not the first time that Oracle has tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.840
form a petty lawsuit. Eversince they started&nbsp;
acquiring other companies in the late 1990s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.780
and especially the 2000s, Oracle has shifted much&nbsp;
of their focus to quote on quote “protecting what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.780
they have” as opposed to creating real value in&nbsp;
the market place. In fact, Oracle has built up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:36.780
history of suing anything that moves, and they’ve&nbsp;
become a dreaded patent troll. In most cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.220
the companies that patent trolls sue simply&nbsp;
give in and come to some sort of settlement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:47.280
because they don’t want to waste more energy or&nbsp;
effort in actually defending themselves. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.580
this was not the same case with Google. For&nbsp;
Google, it wasn't even about the money. If it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.580 --> 00:02:00.060
they could’ve easily settled in the early 2010s&nbsp;
for far less than $9 billion. And this would’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:06.120
likely been cheaper, not to menion less stressful,&nbsp;
than waging an 11 year long battle against Oracle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:12.540
that escalated to the supreme court. But, Google&nbsp;
had no other choice because Oracle wasn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:18.000
targeting Google. Oracle was looking to set a&nbsp;
precedent that they could sue anyone for using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.820
Java in way that they didn’t like. Also, if&nbsp;
Oracle was able to set this type of precedent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.320
it was just a matter of time until other&nbsp;
companies tried to set similar precedents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.620
with their programming languages whether that&nbsp;
be Typescript, Swift, or C#. So, Google had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.900
to step up to the plate not just for themselves&nbsp;
but for the developer community as a whole. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.760
here’s the story of how Oracle sued Google&nbsp;
only to regret it for the rest of their lives.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.120
ACQUIRING JAVA:
Taking a look back, this whole saga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:59.160
can be traced back to early 2009 when Oracle&nbsp;
started trying to buy out Sun Microsystems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:04.320
If you’re not familiar with Sun Microsystems,&nbsp;
they were a tech giant throughout the 1980s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:10.020
and 90s who did everything from producing computer&nbsp;
hardware to developing programming languages. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.840
were still massive moving into the 2000s, but the&nbsp;
dotcom crash and subsequently the 2008 financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.600
crisis would drag down the company substantially.&nbsp;
Pretty soon, Sun started posting quarterly losses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.600
laying of thousands of employees, and running out&nbsp;
of cash. Normally, such companies would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.840
just go bankrupt and new up and coming companies&nbsp;
would take their place. But, Sun was different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.600
While their business was getting hammered, they&nbsp;
still had a boatload of intellectual property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:46.680
including Java, as they were the creators of Java.&nbsp;
This made them a prime acquisition target, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:52.980
didn’t take long for larger companies like IBM and&nbsp;
Oracle to become quite intrigued. Oracle would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:58.980
up winning the bid for $7.4 billion in April of&nbsp;
2009, and pretty soon, they had their hands on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:05.400
Sun. At first, much of the media speculated that&nbsp;
this was a bold expansion strategy for Oracle as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.440
this would be their first step into hardware.&nbsp;
While this was foreign territory for Oracle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:16.800
it also made sense. Oracle was already the go&nbsp;
to software company for hundreds of thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.920
companies, governments, and academic institutions.&nbsp;
So, maybe they could become the go to hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:28.740
player as well. But, from the very beginning,&nbsp;
Larry Ellison, the founder and CEO of Oracle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:35.040
had his eyes on Java. In fact, he even went as&nbsp;
far as claiming that Java was the single most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:41.220
important software asset they had ever acquired.&nbsp;
Sun’s CEO assumed that this was because Java would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:47.160
allow Oracle to offer enterprise solutions at&nbsp;
much cheaper prices, but Ellison had a much less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.920
honorable plan. Shortly after completing the deal&nbsp;
with Sun, Ellison would turn around and sue Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.920 --> 00:05:00.180
over Android’s use of Java. But, wait a minute,&nbsp;
why didn’t Google just get permission to use Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:06.060
in the first place? Well, first of all, Java is&nbsp;
an open source programming language, and Oracle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:12.180
affirms this themselves in one of their blog&nbsp;
posts. And second of all, Google actually did ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:17.880
for permission as soon as they acquired Android&nbsp;
in 2005. Sun wanted $30 to $50 million in return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.400
for letting Google officially use Java which&nbsp;
really wasn’t a crazy ask. Google was willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:30.300
to pay this no problem, but money wasn’t the only&nbsp;
condition. Sun wanted Google to make all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:36.600
implementations of Java compatible with the Java&nbsp;
virtual machine and other Java programs. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:42.600
a dealbreaker for Google. All Google wanted was&nbsp;
a fork of Java that they could build upon. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:48.300
Sun was basically trying to get Google to&nbsp;
support Java indefinintely. To no surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.700
this deal would fall through, but Google&nbsp;
would move forward nonetheless. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.700 --> 00:06:00.060
Java is opensource, so getting permission was&nbsp;
really just more of a courtesy. Sun knew that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:06.660
couldn’t reasonably challenge Google, so they just&nbsp;
went about their day, but Oracle had other plans.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:10.680
THE BATTLE BEGINS:&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.880
Google went ahead and created their own&nbsp;
programming platform that was strongly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.340
inspired by Java, and they tailored it to be&nbsp;
used with mobile devices, but wait a minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:28.140
what did Google even copy? I mean, you can’t just&nbsp;
copy a programming language itself right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:34.080
that’s right, you can’t really copy a language&nbsp;
itself, but you can copy things that are built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.720
off that language. In the case of Google,&nbsp;
they copied Java’s API packages. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:44.400
not familiar with APIs, they’re basically just&nbsp;
code that can be used to establish communication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:49.980
between servers and devices in a predefined&nbsp;
manner. For example, let’s say you want this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:54.720
cool Twitter integration on your website. You&nbsp;
could try to replicate Twitter’s codebase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:01.320
or you could just call a Twitter API and have&nbsp;
this section appear magically. Java had dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.700
of such APIs that made their virtual machines&nbsp;
and programs substantially more accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:11.760
to developers. Given that Google wanted to make&nbsp;
Android as easy and familiar as Java development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:19.080
they not only copied 37 Java APIs, but they also&nbsp;
gave them the same names. We should note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:24.960
they created way more on their own as their total&nbsp;
library would end up including 168 API packages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:31.020
Not to mention, much of the implementation&nbsp;
code was different as well. But, nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:38.040
Google ended up with 11,500 lines of copied code,&nbsp;
and this would become the basis of Oracle’s entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.900
lawsuit. The lawsuit would make its way to the&nbsp;
jury about a year and a half after the lawsuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:49.080
was filed. At first, the jury had a bit of trouble&nbsp;
coming to a conclusion. They established that yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:54.840
though limited, Google had copied portions of&nbsp;
Java. The real question though was whether this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.840 --> 00:08:00.060
was covered by Fair Use, which was a much&nbsp;
more difficult question to answer. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:04.800
after seeing a couple of presentations from Google&nbsp;
regarding how they modified and altered Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:10.620
the jury would have an easy time siding with&nbsp;
Google. The final verdict was that “As long as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:15.660
specific code written to implement a method is&nbsp;
different, anyone is free under the Copyright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:20.820
Act to write his or her own method to carry out&nbsp;
exactly the same function or specification of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:27.060
any and all methods used in the Java API.” So,&nbsp;
you would think this was an open and shut case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:33.420
right? Well, no as Oracle would of course appeal&nbsp;
which escalated the case to the Federal Circuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:38.760
who looked at the case from a completely different&nbsp;
perspective in early 2014. Instead of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.440
determine whether or not Google’s use was fair,&nbsp;
this court decided to focus on whether APIs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:49.080
were copyrightable. And they would end up ruling&nbsp;
that Oracle’s API packages were indeed creative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:55.920
original, and resembled a taxonomy, and therefore,&nbsp;
they were copyrightable. Google obviously couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.560
accept this, so this time, they would appeal.&nbsp;
Their argument was that the copyrightability of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:06.420
Java was irrelevant. The question should&nbsp;
be whether or not Google’s use was fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:10.860
and the Second District Court would&nbsp;
agree with Google’s stance in 2016.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:18.180
Just because Java was copyrightable did not mean&nbsp;
that Google infringed on said copyright. Now, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:24.180
ball was back in Oracle’s court, and they would of&nbsp;
course appeal once again which takes us to 2018.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:29.220
This time, the court would side with Oracle on the&nbsp;
basis that Google’s use of Java was commercial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:36.420
and thus it was not eligible to be covered by fair&nbsp;
use. At this point, Google had 2 wins and Oracle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:41.580
had 2 wins, and there was only one way to settle&nbsp;
this debate once and for all: the Supreme Court.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:48.420
THE FINAL SHOWDOWN:
This was no longer just a battle between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:54.900
two tech companies playing corporate games. This&nbsp;
was a tech phenomenon as a win for Oracle would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.900 --> 00:10:00.720
usher in an era of restrictive development.&nbsp;
In fact, this battle was so monumental that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.880
the entire tech world would get involved,&nbsp;
and they would all rally behind Google. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:12.060
included Microsoft, Mozilla, Red Hat Inc, IBM, the&nbsp;
Computer &amp; Communications Industry Association,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:19.380
the Internet Association, and even the Auto Care&nbsp;
Association. We also saw 72 intellectual property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:25.020
scholars and 93 computer scientists throw their&nbsp;
hats in for Google. Meanwhile, the only notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:30.960
organization that backed Oracle was the Trump&nbsp;
Organization. And I think the only reason they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:36.540
supported Oracle was because they wanted Oracle&nbsp;
to play into their TikTok takeover plan. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:42.660
little to say, the cards were very much stacked&nbsp;
against Oracle. This is very much how the Supreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:49.140
Court felt as well as they would side with Google&nbsp;
in a 6-2 majority opinion. The bottom line was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:54.780
that copyright laws were created to incentivize&nbsp;
the progress of science and useful arts. The idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:59.700
is that if inventors are able to enjoy exclusive&nbsp;
financial benefits for an extended period of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:05.940
they would be more likely to invent great things.&nbsp;
But, Oracle wasn’t trying to invent or progress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:11.400
They were being patent trolls and preventing&nbsp;
further progress just so that they could make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:17.220
even more money. And therefore, Google’s limited&nbsp;
copying of the Java SE Application Programming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:23.040
Interface constituted a fair use of that material&nbsp;
under copyright law. We should note, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.560
that Justice Clarence Thomas and Samuel Alito&nbsp;
dissented with the majority. They felt that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:34.860
copying Oracle’s code, Google “erased 97.5% of&nbsp;
the value of Oracle’s partnership with Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:40.680
made tens of billions of dollars, and established&nbsp;
its position as the owner of the largest mobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:47.340
operating system in the world.” And thus, Google&nbsp;
should be held accountable for damages. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:52.140
given that 6 justices sided with Google,&nbsp;
that ended up being the final verdict.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:56.040
THE AFTERMATH:&nbsp;

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:02.040
So, in the end, Google came out on top which was&nbsp;
a massive win for the developers and the tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:09.480
industry at large. But, even if Oracle had won the&nbsp;
legal battle, it would’ve been a loss for Oracle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:14.640
as a whole. Here’s the thing, companies don’t pour&nbsp;
billions of dollars into programming languages and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.680
offer them for free out of the goodness of their&nbsp;
hearts. They do this to encourage adoption and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.020
popularity which will in turn give them influence&nbsp;
and control which can then be monetized. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:30.780
creating a programming language or any platform&nbsp;
for that matter is very much a long term game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:35.820
But Oracle got caught up in the short term&nbsp;
and tried to milk Google for fast profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:40.980
and now they’re gonna have to pay for&nbsp;
that indefinitely. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:46.080
companies and developers will be hesitant of&nbsp;
working with Oracle because they don’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:51.300
to get caught up in Google’s situation.&nbsp;
And we can already see the impact. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:57.720
holding the #1 spot in terms of popularity&nbsp;
for over a decade, Java fell to #3. And it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:04.440
likely that this trend will simply continue as the&nbsp;
sentiment is very much turning against Java. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:09.900
it’s not just Oracle’s programming business that&nbsp;
will suffer either. Companies will think twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:15.540
before doing any sort of business with Oracle.&nbsp;
Google, for instance, is slowly switching to SAP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:22.440
and this seems to be a larger industry wide trend.&nbsp;
And given Oracle’s history, you can’t really blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:28.140
them. And that’s how Oracle’s smug decision&nbsp;
to sue Google destroyed their reputation. Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:34.080
you team Google or team Oracle? Comment that&nbsp;
down below. Also, drop a like if you’re not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:39.120
fan of patent trolls. And of course, consider&nbsp;
